A||N||7||Voytek||Please help with A/C problem||||1||
Z||000000||Voytek||07-06-2001||01:40 PM||||Ok.  It's summer and it's hot and I'm planning a trip down to the US of A where it's even hotter.<BR>I installed a belt on my compressor (it was missing before) and plugged it in (the plug was just hanging before).  Now the fan up front runs CONSTANTLY (with or w/o AC on) and the compressor clutch does not engage at all.  I am thinking my AC might work if the compressor works 'cause there seems to still be some pressure in the system.  My questions are:<BR>1.  Why would the fan be running all the time? (probably a short, but where to start?)<BR>2.  Why might the compressor clutch not engage?<BR>3.  Is the compressor a common problem on the Fiero A/C ?<P>Any help will be appreciated!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fiero_24||07-06-2001||02:17 PM||||If the belt was not there, or cut you would now have a seized compressor.  I have a squealing AC belt, and I was going to cut it off until I replaced it (don't need air this week), but was told if I did it would seize up on me.  As for the fan running I am not sure, but I am sure someone in this forum has an answer.  You can always add a switch to the fan up front to turn it off manually.  Hope this helps a little.<P><BR>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT<P>[This message has been edited by Fiero_24 (edited 07-06-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by Fiero_24 (edited 07-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||KissMySSFiero||07-06-2001||02:50 PM||||I dont know about the comp freezing up because you took the belt off.  when i got my car it didnt have a belt on it.  my brother owned the car for about4 yrs and 60K miles.  i got the car with 90K miles on it.  there was no a/c belt the entire time my brother had it(he lived in PA)  well i got the car and lived in fla.  i needed a/c.  i was going to get it fixed then saw i didnt even have a belt.  so i got one.  it worked great.  worked all summer no problems. the following summer i had to replace the evap core.  no bigdeal.  ive had the car for over 3 yrs and it now has almost 165,000 miles and the air works great and i have not had any compressor problems.  <P>now for the compressor not engaging.  the a/c system has a pressure switch on it.  if it doesnt sense there is enough pressure in the system(no freon)  then it wont engage the clutch.  so you still may not have enough freon in your system.  <P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <P>88FORMULA<BR>      <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>       <P>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg">           <BR>"Kids in the backseat cause accidents,  accidents in the backseat caused me!!"||||reg||1
Z||000003||Voytek||07-06-2001||04:36 PM||||thanks for the suggestions, but the compressor used to kick in a while ago (when the belt was still on - before I removed it), even though the A/C did not seem to work.  The pressure in the system is normally low, hence the compressor - it is supposed to build up the pressure, therefore the clutch must engage.  Any other ideas??||||reg||1
Z||000004||Rodrv6||07-06-2001||06:39 PM||||If the pressure of freon in the system gets below a certain point, the compressor will not engage at all.  This is to protect it from trying to run in a nearly empty system.  If you take the plug off of the pressure switch on the reciever/dryer (aluminum can up front) and jumper the terminals in the plug and the compressor engages, then your problem is either very low freon charge or a bad pressure switch.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000005||cadaver||07-07-2001||01:50 PM||||remove the belt and seize the compressor?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fiero_24||07-10-2001||08:00 AM||||I was just told not to take the belt off the compressor.  I was advised by a few peole here at the forum, and my mechanic.  I never heard of it, but I am going on the majority of what I was told.  I was told any extended period of time would ruin the compressor.  Maybe now that I know it won't hurt it I will cut the belt off to keep it from squealing.<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fiero_24||07-12-2001||02:05 PM||||Common sense just struck me about my first reply to this thread.  I was told if you take the belt off you will lock up the compressor.  So what happens if a car sits for a year?  Does the compressor freeze then?  I guess after rethinking it the compressor should be OK.  After thinking that I cut the squealing AC belt off to stop that horrible noise until I can find time to fix it.  <P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||3
